	(text "GPIO MAP -- F13 CLOSED. Every constraint below is a datasheet fact, not a convention.\n\n(a) IO35 / IO36 / IO37 ARE FREE ON THIS MODULE, and only on this module. The datasheet:\n    'For modules with Octal SPI PSRAM, i.e. modules embedded with ESP32-S3R8 or\n    ESP32-S3R16V, pins IO35, IO36 and IO37 are connected to the Octal SPI PSRAM and are\n    NOT AVAILABLE for other uses.' The N8R2 is QUAD PSRAM, so they are ours -- and three\n    power-good sense lines are sitting on them. ORDER AN R8 OR R16V VARIANT AND THREE\n    NETS SHORT INTO THE PSRAM BUS. This is the F38 pinned-PN rule with teeth: the suffix\n    is not a stocking preference, it is a pinout.\n\n(b) IO19 / IO20 CARRY USB D-/D+ AND NOTHING ELSE -- [HARD], CLAUDE.md s4. The stock symbol\n    names pins 13/14 USB_D-/USB_D+ directly, so the reservation is visible on the sheet\n    rather than living in a comment.\n\n(c) STRAPPING PINS: IO0, IO3, IO45, IO46. Nothing that DRIVES during reset is on any of\n    them; each gets a deterministic idle level instead of inheriting an internal weak\n    pull (F21 -- 'floating is a state someone chose'). Datasheet Table 4-1 defaults:\n        IO0  weak pull-UP   (1)  -> 10k to +3V3_D. Momentary BOOT switch pulls it low.\n        IO3  FLOATING       (-)  -> 10k to GND. The only one with NO internal default,\n                                    so it is the one that must not be left to chance.\n        IO45 weak pull-DOWN (0)  -> 10k to GND, matching default (VDD_SPI = 3.3 V).\n        IO46 weak pull-DOWN (0)  -> 10k to GND, matching default (ROM print).\n    The resistors do not CHANGE any default. They make the default survive a neighbouring\n    net, a probe, or a conformal-coat leakage path, which a weak internal pull may not.\n\n(d) IO47 / IO48 carry footnote c and are explicit no-connects."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 619.76 119.38 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "1b7ec494-59ae-5927-abf0-b8587ec208e5")
	)
	(text "USB-C IS A DEBUG PORT. VBUS MUST NEVER MEET +5V_D. THIS IS A RULE, NOT A PREFERENCE.\nVBUS_USB terminates on exactly two things: the ESD array's VBUS pin, and a 100k/47k\nSENSE divider into IO1 (ADC1_CH0) -- 5 V gives 1.60 V at the pin, inside the 3.3 V range,\nat ~34 uA. DETECT ONLY.\nThere is deliberately NO 0R, NO ferrite, NO diode-OR and NO DNP position between VBUS and\nany board rail, because the convenience those buy -- 'just power it from the bench over\nUSB' -- is exactly how two supplies end up back-feeding each other through a part nobody\nre-examined. The board is powered from VBATT_PROT. A stuffing option here would be a\nloaded gun with a DNP label on it.\nConsequence to design AROUND, not to fix here: with USB attached and the vehicle off, the\nmodule is unpowered and the port is dead. That is correct. Bench work uses a 12 V supply.\n\nCC1/CC2 get 5.1k Rd EACH -- two resistors, not one. The port is a device (UFP) and must\npresent Rd on whichever CC line the cable orientation lands on; a single resistor works\none way round and reads as an unattached port the other, which presents as an intermittent\ncable rather than as a design error.\nSBU1/SBU2 are unused and explicitly no-connected (F28).\n\nESD: USBLC6-2SC6 on D+/D- with its VBUS pin tied to VBUS_USB. The connector faces the\noutside world exactly as VBATT does -- politer, but the same class of exposure, and the\nsame reasoning that put a TVS on the battery input applies here at 1/1000th the energy."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 619.76 299.72 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "db57a3a6-c4f1-5924-b6aa-e7189f21bd6f")
	)
	(text "WHAT THIS SHEET OWES THE SEQUENCE (S7.2 / S7.3), drawn as NAMED NETS so BRINGUP.md stays\nmechanically checkable against copper rather than by reading:\n  PG_5V_D  PG_6V2_A  PG_1V2  PG_13V5P  PG_13V5N   -- rail readiness, all open-drain,\n                                                     pulled up here where the reader is\n  RAILMON_ALERT  TEMP_ALERT_N                     -- I2C monitor pair interrupts\n  I2C0_SDA / I2C0_SCL                             -- bus pull-ups live HERE, once, with\n                                                     the controller. Do not add more at\n                                                     the far end; two pull-up pairs on one\n                                                     bus is a rise-time bug that measures\n                                                     fine and fails at temperature.\n  DSP_RESET_N  ADC_PDN_N  DAC1_PDN_N  DAC2_PDN_N  -- step 3 and step 5 actors\n  ADC_OVF                                         -- F29 clip flag, edge interrupt\n  MUTE_EN                                         -- series photoMOS drive. ENERGISED to\n                                                     pass: S7.4's zero-energy rule means\n                                                     this net doing nothing IS mute.\n  REMOTE_IN_SENSE  REMOTE_OUT_EN                  -- steps 1 and 6\n  BM83_UART_TXD/RXD  BM83_MFB                     -- S5 host-mode control plane (F32)\n  SPI_DSP_*                                       -- step 4 slave boot, 20 MHz capable\n\nThe 12.5 ms bus-quiet window (F30) is FIRMWARE, not copper, and it is in BRINGUP.md. It\ncannot be enforced by the schematic -- which is exactly why it is written down twice."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 619.76 430.53000000000003 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "dba2eb3f-df5e-5911-ad4c-02d9f62a7120")
	)
	(embedded_fonts no)
)
